## A one-year bet on San Diego

After that year of loss, Viola made a decision most people would only daydream about. She sold her house in Sarasota, Florida, with everything in it, and moved to San Diego.

"I gave myself a year," she says. "And I said, if it works, great. And if not, I know how to get a job."

It's coming up on four years now, and it's working.

"In my opinion, San Diego is the most amazing, most beautiful place that you could ever possibly live," she says. "The weather, the energy, the people." She made more friends in her first year here, she says, than in the entire decade she lived in Sarasota. "It's just that I'm where I belong, and when you are where you belong, your roots can grow and you can flourish."

Viola is based in La Jolla and splits her home time between Southern California and suburban Philadelphia. She travels constantly for work, "everywhere from Sitka, Alaska, to Miami, Florida, and everything in between." But a San Diego booking is still her favorite. "When I get to do an engagement right here in my hometown, it's such a joy for me."

## From the anchor desk to QVC

Her résumé reads like several careers. She has been a news anchor, an award-winning public information officer, a QVC show host and a builder of workplace cultures. One of her favorite lessons came from the moment her path took an unexpected turn.

Early on, she was a news anchor and reporter at a very small station in New Jersey, headed, she thought, toward broadcast journalism on a major scale. Someone whose opinion she valued saw that QVC was holding open-call auditions. Knowing she was barely scraping by on a small-market salary, they urged her to try out.

So she did, on a whim. She crushed the audition, partly, she thinks, because she didn't want the job. She went back for a second interview as a good exercise in speaking off the cuff. By the third, she planned to let them down gently.

Then they made her an offer: triple her salary, a wardrobe allowance, and regular hair and nail appointments.

"As a 25-year-old, to me it was like, holy cow! I just hit the jackpot," she says. "So broadcast journalism went out the window. All of a sudden, I was all about selling gold chains and Capodimonte at 2 o'clock in the morning."

People love that story, she says. But she also tells it as a warning.

"When you abandon what you really want to make other people happy, you end up living your life for other people instead of yourself," she says. "There is one person you're guaranteed to spend the rest of your life with, and it's you. So start listening to yourself."

