## A keynote for a moment of fear

Viola offers a catalog of keynotes on courage, burnout, empathy, culture and self-worth. Right now the one everybody is asking for is "Embrace the Shift: Turning Uncertainty into Opportunity."

She built it for organizations where change, and especially AI, has people worried about their jobs.

"When fear is in a culture, it shuts down creativity, curiosity, and innovation," she says. The talk pairs science-based tools with real-life stories, because, as she puts it, "I have to live it to give it."

Her message isn't that fear goes away. "Fear is a part of life," she says. "The question is: Are you putting it in the driver's seat, or is it just a passenger?" She encourages audiences to approach life more like scientists, who expect failure because they know it's the path to breakthroughs, and to stop "awfulizing," which she describes as filling in the gaps with negative stories that make you shut down.

If you'd asked her six months ago which talk mattered most, she says, she would have named a different one. She thinks of her keynotes and books as her children, each one born out of a need she sees in her clients. "Sometimes I'll build something special for them that ends up being useful to many other organizations."

## "Until you learn how to say no"

The biggest "aha" moments in her rooms come, she says, when people realize they are "the CEO of themselves," the gatekeepers of their own well-being.

"Until you learn how to say no, your yeses will have no meaning," she tells audiences.

That doesn't mean saying no "like a jerk," she says. "You can say no with grace." But when you start to value and protect your own time and energy, she says, respect for you grows, and so does your executive presence.

She didn't learn it easily. "I burned myself out for many years because I was the people pleaser," she says. "I felt like if I could make everybody else happy, that surely I would be happy. And what I realized, I was just draining my tank."

She shared lessons like these recently with the Women in Business group at San Diego State University, telling a room of young women all the things she wishes someone had told her. "It's the school of hard knocks," she says, "but it's not a failure if you've learned something from it, and I have learned from every failure. In fact, I say my messes are my message."

