## "Don't borrow tomorrow's heartache"

Viola also works one-on-one with executives, and one line comes up again and again.

"Don't borrow tomorrow's heartache," she says.

She'll be mind-mapping a problem with a leader when the conversation slides into what-ifs: if this happens, then that could happen, and then that. She watches their energy drop over a problem that isn't real yet. So she stops and asks what the odds really are that the first thing will happen. If it's anything under 80%, she tells them they're borrowing heartache.

"When it's real, you will have the skills to deal with it," she says. "But let's deal with what's actually real and what's on your plate."

She likes to quote a line often attributed to Mark Twain: "I lived through many terrible things, and some of them actually happened." And she reminds clients that what-ifs can work in both directions. "Flip the script, because your what-ifs also can lead to your biggest dreams come true too," she says.

## Standing up for public servants

Viola's background as a public information officer shows up in one of her best-known books, The Public Servants' Survival Guide. She wrote it, she says, because she saw public servants "burning out left and right." The book lays out 10 warning signs of burnout with ways to course-correct, and its lessons are the basis of her Beyond Burnout keynote and workshops she has delivered around the country.

Ask her what people outside government misunderstand, and she doesn't hesitate.

"They are some of the most hardworking people I've ever met," she says. "They don't necessarily have the perks or the salary of those that are in the private sector." She points to the crews filling potholes, the clerks who make sure you get your public record, and the police on patrol. "These are selfless people."

She also wants people to know that many firefighters across the country are volunteers. "If there is something going on in the middle of the Super Bowl, they have to leave their Barcalounger and run and respond," she says. "They don't have to do this, and yet they do."

## How Vici works with clients

Vici Communications coordinates Brenda's keynotes along with her advisory, consulting and coaching work. That includes corporate training, culture consulting and one-on-one executive coaching, including presentation coaching for leaders who need to deliver a high-stakes talk. Beyond The Public Servants’ Survival Guide, she has written a communication book, How to Be Heard Without Screaming!, and a children's book, Annabelle the Octopus, a story about friendship and asking for help.

She doesn't give "canned" speeches. She spends time with each client to understand the audience and its pain points, then shapes the message to fit.

And if you run a San Diego organization, she'd love to hear from you.

"If any of your listeners need either corporate training, keynotes, culture consulting, or even one-on-one executive coaching," she says, "I would love to serve them."

